// Common CR Reference — the curation worklist (SkyCall, Session 152, 2026-08-13).
//
// STATIC consumer asset (NOT regenerated by excel_to_dashboard.py), lazy-loaded
// by the inline boot in the tab shell on first activation of #tab-cr-reference.
//
// WHY THIS IS A WORKLIST AND NOT A MERGE ENGINE
// ---------------------------------------------
// Sam asked for "a Common CR Reference just as we have pretty well developed
// CER, CSR, and the beginnings of a CCR", and ruled that C-ID is only ONE
// factor — "similar to the CCR, we take into account matching factors like
// title, course name and number, course description, subject, etc."
//
// docs/common_cr_reference_scope.md measured what automation can reach against
// those factors: rung 1 published statewide set, rung 2 C-ID, rung 3 CCR course
// identity, rung 4 mechanical twin merge — **about a tenth of 2,344 strings.**
// The other nine tenths is curator judgement and no achievable matcher changes
// it, because "Racial Issues and the Police", "Community Relations" and
// "Community and the Justice System" are one POST topic in three unrelated sets
// of words. So the queue, the attribution and the receipt are the product; the
// matcher is the small automated spine underneath it.
//
// SCOPE IS GLOBAL, WITH A SPLIT AFFORDANCE (Sam, 2026-08-13). One vocabulary
// across all credentials; the credential rides along as context; a curator can
// split a wording out of a group where the meaning genuinely differs.
//
// WHAT MAKES THE QUEUE TRACTABLE. The corpus is head-heavy — measured, not
// assumed: the top 50 strings carry 49.4% of all articulation rows, and only
// 156 groups carry a decision at all. This is an afternoon, not an ocean.
//
// TEAM-GATED: internal curation material, and the decisions table is RLS-gated
// anyway, so a logged-out visitor would see a shell with no decisions in it.
